{"name":"Temple Tile","hex":"#a9855d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"temple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A house of worship, especially:\nA house of worship dedicated to a polytheistic faith.","A house of worship dedicated to a polytheistic faith."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To build a temple for; to appropriate a temple to; to temple a god"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/temple"},{"term":"tile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A regularly-shaped slab of clay or other material, affixed to cover or decorate a surface, as in a roof-tile, glazed tile, stove tile, carpet tile, etc.","A rectangular graphic."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with tiles.","To arrange in a regular pattern, with adjoining edges (applied to tile-like objects, graphics, windows in a computer interface)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Temple","blocks":[{"text":"A temple is a place of worship, a building used for spiritual rituals and activities such as prayer and sacrifice. By convention, the specially built places of worship for some religions are commonly called \"temples\" in English, while those of other religions are not, even though they fulfill very similar functions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Philae_temple_at_night.jpg/330px-Philae_temple_at_night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple","wikidata":"Q44539"},{"title":"Tile","blocks":[{"text":"Tiles are usually thin, square rectangular coverings manufactured from hard-wearing material such as ceramic, stone, metal, baked clay, or even glass. They are generally fixed in place in an array to cover roofs, floors, walls, edges, or other objects such as tabletops. Alternatively, tile can sometimes refer to similar units made from lightweight materials such as perlite, wood, and mineral wool, typically used for wall and ceiling applications. In another sense, a tile is a construction tile or similar object, such as rectangular counters used in playing games. The word is derived from the French word tuile, which is, in turn, from the Latin word tegula, meaning a roof tile composed of fired clay, otherwise known as nga."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Alban_chambon%2C_decorazione_in_marmo_e_ceramica%2C_dal_castello_reale_delle_ardenne%2C_houyet%2C_1905_ca._02.jpg/330px-Alban_chambon%2C_decorazione_in_marmo_e_ceramica%2C_dal_castello_reale_delle_ardenne%2C_houyet%2C_1905_ca._02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tile","wikidata":"Q468402"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ankor_Wat_temple.jpg/500px-Ankor_Wat_temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ankor_Wat_temple.jpg/500px-Ankor_Wat_temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 12th-century Angkor Wat temple complex in Cambodia is the largest religious structure in the world[1] and is dedicated to the Hindu deity Vishnu.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnkor_Wat_temple.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Panoramic_views_of_Borobudur.jpg/500px-Panoramic_views_of_Borobudur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Panoramic_views_of_Borobudur.jpg/1280px-Panoramic_views_of_Borobudur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Panoramic_views_of_Borobudur.jpg/500px-Panoramic_views_of_Borobudur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th